        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix: Node allowlist writeback can restore revoked exec approvals",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nExec-approval mutations were not serialized across every writer. A runtime usage update, compatibility helper, or macOS settings edit could save a stale snapshot after an administrator revoked an allowlist entry, restoring execution permission that was meant to stay revoked.\n\nCloses #78225.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\n- Serialize Node and macOS approval mutations through the same sidecar lock.\n- Re-read current policy inside the lock and apply only the intended entry-scoped mutation.\n- Preserve the shipped synchronous compatibility APIs as synchronous, durable writes.\n- Persist generated macOS allowlist IDs before the UI performs its first entry-scoped edit.\n- Fail closed for malformed, ownerless, reused-PID, or otherwise unverifiable lock ownership; pathname-based stale-lock deletion cannot safely distinguish a replacement lock.\n\n## User Impact\n\nRevoked approvals remain revoked when runtime metadata updates or Mac settings edits race with administrative policy changes. Contended or unverifiable lock state now produces a bounded failure instead of risking a stale policy overwrite.\n\n## Change Type\n\n- [x] Bug fix\n- [ ] Feature\n- [x] Refactor required for the fix\n- [ ] Docs\n- [x] Security hardening\n- [ ] Chore/infra\n\n## Scope\n\n- [x] Gateway / orchestration\n- [x] Skills / tool execution\n- [ ] Auth / tokens\n- [x] Memory / storage\n- [ ] Integrations\n- [x] API / contracts\n- [x] UI / DX\n- [ ] CI/CD / infra\n\n## Root Cause\n\nSeveral execution-time helpers mutated an approvals object derived from an earlier policy-evaluation snapshot and wrote it without participating in the same cross-process serialization boundary as administrative writes. macOS settings also replaced whole allowlist arrays from UI snapshots. Hash checks narrowed some races but did not make the complete read-modify-write operation atomic.\n\n## Implementation\n\n- Centralize approval read-modify-write transactions in `src/infra/exec-approvals.ts`.\n- Add a synchronous lock path for the already-shipped synchronous compatibility surface.\n- Keep runtime metadata and allow-always decisions atomic with the current on-disk policy.\n- Give the Mac app the same lock identity format and entry-scoped update/remove operations.\n- Add lock-owner PID start-time checks for Linux and macOS while deliberately refusing non-atomic stale-lock unlink.\n- Make the public plugin file-lock wrapper fail closed on definitely stale owners until the underlying lock primitive can provide atomic replacement-safe recovery.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Fresh independent autoreview: no accepted/actionable findings; overall correctness 0.87.\n- `git diff --check`: passed at exact head `c779185bd94e2a1dda53ecbbac85a896eba4d52f`.\n- Added regression coverage for stale snapshot revocation, concurrent-process contention, reused PIDs, malformed/ownerless locks, legacy idless Mac entries, and entry-scoped Mac edits.\n- Sanitized exact-head AWS Crabbox tests and macOS CI are in progress; final run IDs and results will be posted before landing.\n\n## Security Impact\n\n- New permissions/capabilities? No.\n- Secrets/tokens handling changed? No.\n- New/changed network calls? No.\n- Command/tool execution surface changed? Yes: approval persistence now fails closed under unsafe concurrency.\n- Data access scope changed? No.\n\n## Compatibility / Migration\n\n- Backward compatible: Yes.\n- Config/env changes: No.\n- Migration needed: No.\n- Existing synchronous approval helper contracts remain synchronous and durable.\n\n## Risks and Mitigations\n\n- Risk: an unverifiable lock can temporarily block approval mutation.\n  - Mitigation: bounded retry for valid live cross-process contention; fail-closed errors preserve policy integrity.\n- Risk: lock behavior is shared by Node and macOS.\n  - Mitigation: both implementations use the same lock path and identity shape, with platform-specific regression tests.\n- Risk: deliberately disabling pathname-based stale-lock deletion can require operator cleanup after a crash.\n  - Mitigation: this avoids deleting a replacement live lock; atomic stale recovery is being handled at the lock-library owner boundary.\n\n## Human Verification\n\n- Verified by review: all current mutation entry points use either the serialized transaction or explicit whole-file administrative replacement.\n- Not yet verified: final exact-head remote test run and GitHub macOS lane; both are required before merge.\n\n## Review Conversations\n\n- [ ] I replied to or resolved every bot review conversation I addressed in this PR.\n- [ ] I left unresolved only conversations that still need reviewer or maintainer judgment.\n",
